Hi,

please find my comments below.
Il 08/09/2026 16:35, Gould, James ha scritto:

I'm replying to my own message to answer the question "Should the new languag=
e be retroactively applied to existing working group documents (draft-ietf-re=
gext-epp-https)?".  The answer to this question for draft-ietf-regext-epp-htt=
ps should be "no", based on the following:



1. draft-ietf-regext-epp-https was adopted by REGEXT on 2/2025 based on an ea=
rlier charter (Charter 02).  A question was raised on the mailing list whethe=
r the charter's scope supported the transport drafts (EoH in draft-ietf-regex=
t-epp-https) and EoQ in draft-ietf-regext-epp-quic), which was determined to =
be in-scope and the transport drafts were adopted.

2. The charter was updated to clarify that EoH and EoQ are in scope in Charte=
r 03, that was approved on 7/2026.

3. The new charter (Charter 04), approved on 9/2026, was described as "Minor =
reframing of Charter to better align with general pattern of OPS area Charter=
s", where the language "New application transport mappings for EPP following =
relevant and appropriate IETF standards to address operational gaps for which=
 at least one domain registry and at least one domain registrar have made a c=
ommitment to deploy (Standards Track or Experimental)" is a material change t=
hat adds a new obligation applicable to a multistakeholder governance model.

4. The "commitment to deploy" language is unique to the REGEXT charter and ne=
eds further review and discussion on the mailing list that is independent of =
the draft-ietf-regext-epp-https work.



Thanks,

[ML] I believe the phrase "New application transport mappings for EPP ..." ap=
plies more naturally when RegExt evaluates the adoption of additional EPP tra=
nsport mappings. IMO, there is no indication that this specific revision of t=
he charter was intended to reopen the previously reached consensus on EoH or =
to impose a new adoption criterion for an existing WG document.

Furthermore, EPP over HTTPS is not merely a hypothetical deployment model. Re=
gistro .it has operated an HTTPS-based EPP server since 2009, and .it registr=
ars have used this transport approach for many years. Some of the operational=
 considerations outlined in the draft stem from the experience gained over th=
is period. Although the existing .it implementation slightly differs from dra=
ft-ietf-regext-epp-https - notably, when the HTTP session is established - it=
 provides substantial operational evidence that both the registry and its reg=
istrars have accepted EPP over HTTPS as a viable deployment model.

Against that background, requiring a registrar to make a formal "commitment t=
o deploy" merely because the charter language changed after adoption of the d=
ocument would add little evidence of actual deployability. It would amount to=
 imposing a recently introduced procedural formality on activities for which =
long-standing operational procedures and registrar acceptance already exist.



Best,

Mario

